Announce

Payload layout and validation. For what announces are for and how they
propagate, see upstream docs/source/understanding.rst, section "The
Announce Mechanism in Detail".

Vectors: test/announce/

An announce is a packet of packet type announce. It is not encrypted.
See doc/packet for the header.


Payload

    offset  size  field
    0       64    public key
    64      10    name hash
    74      10    random hash
    84      32    ratchet, only when the context flag is set
    84/116  64    signature
    148/180 -     app data, optional

Without a ratchet the payload is at least 148 bytes, with one at least
180. Anything beyond the signature is app data.

    RNS/Destination.py#announce_data, RNS/Identity.py#validate_announce


Random hash

Ten bytes, in two parts:

    offset  size  field
    0       5     random
    5       5     unix seconds, big endian

The timestamp is truncated to five bytes, not five bytes of a larger
field. The same destination announcing twice therefore produces
different bytes, which is why no announce vector can be produced a
second time. It can still be rebuilt: expect records the ten bytes as
they came out, so announce vectors are encode class.

    RNS/Destination.py#get_random_hash


Signed data

    signed_data = destination_hash || public_key || name_hash
                  || random_hash || ratchet || app_data

Two things differ from the wire layout, and both are easy to get
wrong.

The destination hash is signed but is not part of the payload. It is
taken from the packet header.

App data is transmitted after the signature but signed before it. On
the wire it follows the signature; in the signed data it precedes it.

Every announce vector carries the assembled signed_data field followed
by the signature_valid verdict. An implementation whose verification
fails can compare its assembly before suspecting its curve code.

test/announce/altered-signature is a valid announce with one signature
byte altered. It decodes cleanly and its destination hash matches; only
the signature fails. test/announce/destination-mismatch fails both
checks, because the destination hash is part of the signed data.

    sender    RNS/Destination.py#signed_data
    receiver  RNS/Identity.py#signed_data


Validation

The reference implementation checks, in this order:

    1  the signature over signed_data, using the announced public key
    2  the destination hash

The second check is

    identity_hash = SHA-256(public_key)[0:16]
    expected_hash = SHA-256(name_hash || identity_hash)[0:16]
    expected_hash == destination_hash

The announce carries the name hash but not the name. A receiver
therefore learns which destination hash is claimed and can confirm it
belongs to the announced key, but cannot recover the aspect names.

    RNS/Identity.py#hash_material, RNS/Identity.py#expected_hash


Ratchet

Thirty-two bytes, an X25519 public key, present when and only when the
context flag in the header is set. A receiver that ignores the context
flag will read the first 32 bytes of the ratchet as part of the
signature and reject every ratchet announce.

    RNS/Destination.py#_ratchet_public_bytes, RNS/Identity.py#FLAG_SET


Reproducibility of the vectors

The announces in this corpus were generated with the clock and both
randomness sources pinned, so that regenerating produces identical
bytes. make verify enforces it: gen exits non-zero if any committed
byte would change.

Both sources are pinned on RNS itself. Pinning os.urandom is not
enough and was wrong here for a while: under the openssl backend
_generate_ratchet reaches X25519PrivateKey.generate in PyCA
(RNS/Cryptography/Proxies.py#generate), which draws from OpenSSL's own
generator and never touches os.urandom, so the two ratchet vectors
changed on every run while this section claimed they did not.

That is a property of tools/gen, not of the protocol. It is also not
what the encode class turns on. Consumers cannot sign a fresh announce
from these vectors, because raw carries no private key; what they can
do is lay the fields back out into the packet they came from, and
cmd/check requires it of every announce that decodes.
